**[Slide 1: Introduction & Objective]**

Thank you for your time today. I will be walking you through our Claim Fraud Analytics program for personal property and casualty claims in North America. This initiative is sponsored by Chris and delivered by the Fraud Analytics Center of Excellence.

In any claims analytics environment, the core goal is straightforward: use AI to find suspicious claims earlier in the lifecycle, while we still have the leverage to act on them. Our objective is to give the system the ability to spot and prioritize high-risk claims right at intake, well before we reach settlement.

This matters for three key reasons: First, we pay out less on fraudulent claims. Second, we spend less time and money chasing recoveries after the fact. And third, we inherently protect the profitability of our broader claims portfolio.

**[Slide 2: Current vs. Future State]**

Where do we stand today? Currently, our fraud models are reactive. They look back at claims that have already been paid and flag them for recovery. While that is better than nothing, the reality is that once the money has left the door, recovering it is expensive, time-consuming, and highly uncertain.

This is the exact gap we are closing.

Our future state is completely proactive. We are entirely redesigning the models with AI and natively integrating them into Claim Vision and Claim Connect. This means scoring happens seamlessly inside normal claims workflows. When a claim looks suspicious, the integration creates an immediate alert for the SIU, helping them triage it *before* settlement.

In parallel, we are building a Context Model powered by a Large Language Model. Rather than relying on simple keyword matching, it is designed to find complex, richer patterns in exactly how a claim is described. We are on track to deliver this in Q4 of 2026.

